  "method": "Owned A and B lab emulators. A placed voice and video calls to B in their direct chat, and B placed one call to A. Each call was answered and ended, left ringing, cancelled by the caller, or declined by B. A private probe on B's profile read the resulting chat logs with SYNCMSG. Raw attachments contained call-server addresses and a call token; they were reduced to their key set, event type and duration, then deleted. The attachments here are synthetic with the observed key set. android_receiver_bubble is the text B's Android client displayed.",
  "doc": "research/calls.md"
 },
 "notes": "Every call produced two type-51 chat logs, both authored by the caller: the start (invite or v_invite) when the call was placed, then the outcome when it ended. The chat log's message field held the same JSON as its attachment. duration is whole seconds and was non-zero only for bye and v_bye. The video call's 21 matched both phones' call timers; the answered voice call ended when both emulators hung mid-call, so its 80 has no on-screen timer to compare against.",
